[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports say otherwise. Every report
is about a date on the wrong day, but they hold three different problems. One fix closes only the
first.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix (the shared parser) will not close every report in #4471. Two problems have no
ticket of their own until the tickets below are filed.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} />

The counts above are computed from the export at render time, not typed.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** A tenant whose locale writes the
day first gets its dates flipped. This is what #4471 is about, and the queued fix covers it. It
is the large majority of the reports, reported through the app, email and the form on `booking-v2`.

**`hint`: the `booking-v3` form tells people to type `MM/DD/YYYY`.** Three tenants run that
form (`alder.physio`, `copper.studio`, `pale.harbour`) and all three write the day first. Their
people type what the hint says, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. Only reports
that came through the form on those tenants belong here. A parser fix does not change what the
hint tells people to type. Fixing the parser alone will leave these reports open.

**`clock`: the invite moves by one day, not by a flipped month.** The reports describe a
weekday that is off by one (Tuesday became Monday), an invite that arrived before the booking
was made, and the same meeting landing on different days for Lisbon and Toronto. That points at
time zones or a date boundary, not at day and month order. The desk already marked these reports
as "read as a one-day move" in `desk-notes.json`, and filed them under `parser` anyway. The cause
is still a guess: this ticket needs an engineer to look.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone. Subjects
here are generic ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so all three problems share them.
Intake does not help either: its tag guesses from the reporter's browser and not from the
tenant, so it disagrees with the cause for a large part of the reports. The cause depends on the
tenant (the locale it writes dates in, and the booking form it runs), as
[`causes.ts`](https://github.com/a-boro/mega_dev_kb_s01e03_challenge/blob/main/src/lib/kb/causes.ts)
already does.

The export also repeats some reports: its pages overlap, so a report can come twice. The counts
here use one row per id.

## What to do

- Keep #4471 for `parser` and let the queued fix close it ([ticket 1](https://github.com/a-boro/mega_dev_kb_s01e03_challenge/issues/1)).
- `hint` has its own ticket ([ticket 2](https://github.com/a-boro/mega_dev_kb_s01e03_challenge/issues/2)), and so does `clock` ([ticket 3](https://github.com/a-boro/mega_dev_kb_s01e03_challenge/issues/3)).
- Count reports per cause, not per subject, when you merge the next big incident.

